\insts is used to initialize or add instruments.
Calling this command is necessary for a LiveLily session
to work. To initialize instruments, call it with the names
you want to give to the instruments, without the
backslash, like below:

\insts.init inst1 inst2

Once created, the arguments passed to \insts.init works
like a command itself, by prepending the backslash, like

\inst1

Initializing instruments is possible only once per session.
If you want to add instruments after you have initialized
some, call \insts.add like below:

\insts.add inst3

Each defined instrument has its own commands and/or
arguments.

To turn an instrument to a rhythmic one, call:

\inst1 rhythm

This will change its staff to a one-liner where you can
write rhythmic patterns.

A rhythmic instrument can also be written in the x - notation,
where every x is a hit and every - a rest, each one step of
\score.seqres long (16ths by default). Steps can be separate
or joined, and mixed:

\inst1 x - x x - - x - x-x-x-x-

X is an accented hit, and a hit takes articulations,
dynamics, and text as notes do (e.g. x-. or x\f). A -
followed by an articulation symbol is an articulation, and
any other - is a rest, so x-- is a hit and two rests. The
x - notation can't be used by instruments that are not
rhythmic.

To transpose the notes that appear on the score (for
example for a clarinet or a saxophone), call:

\inst2.transpose -3

The number defines how many semitones the notes on the
score will be transposed.

If you want to send the instrument's notes via OSC but
as MIDI notes, call:

\inst3.sendmidi

Some instruments sound late, like robotic acoustic
instruments. For example, a Yamaha Disklavier needs about
500ms to sound its notes. To set this delay, in
milliseconds, call:

\inst1.delay 500

The sequencer sends the notes of such an instrument this
much time earlier, so that they sound in time with the
score and the instruments without a delay. When the
sequencer starts, the instrument with the longest delay
starts first, then the one with the second longest delay,
and so on, and the score and the instruments without a
delay start after the longest delay. The notes of an
instrument with a delay are highlighted in the score and
in the editor when they sound, and so are the functions
bound to it called.
A delay of 0 removes it, and the delay can't be negative.
While the sequencer plays, a new delay applies from the
start of the next bar. A loop called during the last
moments of a bar reaches an instrument with a delay in the
middle of its next bar, as its notes have been sent
already, so the instrument starts from the point it should
be at.

To turn the LiveLily-part program of an instrument
fullscreen (not in the computer that runs this LiveLily
instance, but a computer that displays part of the full
score), call:

\inst1.fullscreen on

To switch back to a smaller window, call the command with
off.

To hide the cursor in a LiveLily-part program, call:

\inst1.cursor hide

To bring the cursor back, call it with show.
